    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old sustains a fall and suffers a periprosthetic femur fracture around their total hip arthroplasty. Radiographs show the fracture is Around the stem, stem is loose. This is classified as Vancouver B2. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Nonoperative management with protected weight-bearing", "Revision arthroplasty with a longer stem", "Revision arthroplasty with proximal femoral replacement", "ORIF with intramedullary nailing", "ORIF with plates and cables"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Vancouver classification dictates treatment of postoperative periprosthetic femur fractures. Vancouver B2 fractures occur Around the stem, stem is loose and are best managed by Revision arthroplasty with a longer stem.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old sustains a fall and suffers a periprosthetic femur fracture around their total hip arthroplasty. Radiographs show the fracture is Trochanteric region. This is classified as Vancouver A. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Revision arthroplasty with a longer stem", "Nonoperative or cerclage wires if widely displaced", "Nonoperative management with protected weight-bearing", "Revision arthroplasty with proximal femoral replacement", "ORIF with plates and cables"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The Vancouver classification dictates treatment of postoperative periprosthetic femur fractures. Vancouver A fractures occur Trochanteric region and are best managed by Nonoperative or cerclage wires if widely displaced.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old patient is undergoing a total joint arthroplasty. The surgical team is reviewing the risk of perioperative systemic complications. Regarding myocardial infarction, which of the following is most accurate in this setting?', '["It only occurs after the 3-month postoperative mark", "It is solely caused by the type of implant bearing surface used", "Within the first 30 days postoperatively", "It is completely preventable with prophylactic antibiotics", "It is a rare complication seen almost exclusively in young patients"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Myocardial infarction is a known systemic complication following major joint arthroplasty. Specifically, it is within the first 30 days postoperatively. Proper medical optimization and perioperative care are crucial for risk mitigation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-year-old patient with end-stage post-traumatic ankle osteoarthritis is seeking surgical intervention. Which of the following patient profiles is considered the MOST ideal candidate for a total ankle arthroplasty (TAA) rather than an ankle arthrodesis?', '["An older, lower-demand patient with relatively good bone stock and normal hindfoot alignment", "A patient with severe avascular necrosis of the talus", "A patient with a history of deep soft-tissue infection in the affected ankle", "A patient with marked, uncorrectable varus deformity of the hindfoot", "A young, high-demand laborer with a physically strenuous job"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Total ankle arthroplasty is generally preferred for older, lower-demand individuals who wish to preserve range of motion. Contraindications typically include high physical demand, severe malalignment, prior infection, and poor bone stock (e.g., extensive AVN of the talus). Arthodesis remains the gold standard for high-demand patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old patient with end-stage post-traumatic ankle osteoarthritis is seeking surgical intervention. Which of the following patient profiles is considered the MOST ideal candidate for a total ankle arthroplasty (TAA) rather than an ankle arthrodesis?', '["A patient with a history of deep soft-tissue infection in the affected ankle", "A patient with severe avascular necrosis of the talus", "An older, lower-demand patient with relatively good bone stock and normal hindfoot alignment", "A patient with marked, uncorrectable varus deformity of the hindfoot", "A young, high-demand laborer with a physically strenuous job"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Total ankle arthroplasty is generally preferred for older, lower-demand individuals who wish to preserve range of motion. Contraindications typically include high physical demand, severe malalignment, prior infection, and poor bone stock (e.g., extensive AVN of the talus). Arthodesis remains the gold standard for high-demand patients.'),
