    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old patient presents with severe shoulder pain and limited range of motion. Imaging and clinical evaluation confirm massive irreparable rotator cuff tear with pseudoparalysis (cuff tear arthropathy). Which of the following is the most appropriate surgical intervention?', '["Anatomic Total Shoulder Arthroplasty (TSA)", "Hemiarthroplasty", "Arthrodesis", "Arthroscopic debridement and subacromial decompression", "Reverse Total Shoulder Arthroplasty (rTSA)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The primary indication for Reverse Total Shoulder Arthroplasty (rTSA) is massive irreparable rotator cuff tear with pseudoparalysis (cuff tear arthropathy). Anatomic TSA relies on an intact rotator cuff to maintain the humeral head centered on the glenoid. In contrast, rTSA is designed for cuffs that are severely deficient, utilizing the deltoid muscle to substitute for the rotator cuff.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old patient with end-stage post-traumatic ankle osteoarthritis is seeking surgical intervention. Which of the following patient profiles is considered the MOST ideal candidate for a total ankle arthroplasty (TAA) rather than an ankle arthrodesis?', '["An older, lower-demand patient with relatively good bone stock and normal hindfoot alignment", "A patient with marked, uncorrectable varus deformity of the hindfoot", "A patient with severe avascular necrosis of the talus", "A young, high-demand laborer with a physically strenuous job", "A patient with a history of deep soft-tissue infection in the affected ankle"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Total ankle arthroplasty is generally preferred for older, lower-demand individuals who wish to preserve range of motion. Contraindications typically include high physical demand, severe malalignment, prior infection, and poor bone stock (e.g., extensive AVN of the talus). Arthodesis remains the gold standard for high-demand patients.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old patient presents with a painful left hip and a palpable soft-tissue mass. They underwent a metal-on-metal total hip arthroplasty 10 years ago. Serum cobalt and chromium levels are elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Adverse local tissue reaction (ALTR) / Pseudotumor", "Periprosthetic joint infection", "Iliopsoas bursitis", "Heterotopic ossification", "Aseptic loosening due to polyethylene wear"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Metal-on-metal hip implants can produce metal ion wear debris, which can trigger an adverse local tissue reaction (ALTR), often manifesting as a solid or cystic pseudotumor, pain, and elevated serum metal ions.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old patient is undergoing a total joint arthroplasty. The surgical team is reviewing the risk of perioperative systemic complications. Regarding delirium, which of the following is most accurate in this setting?', '["It only occurs after the 3-month postoperative mark", "It is completely preventable with prophylactic antibiotics", "Common in elderly patients, associated with narcotic use and hypoxia", "It is solely caused by the type of implant bearing surface used", "It is a rare complication seen almost exclusively in young patients"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Delirium is a known systemic complication following major joint arthroplasty. Specifically, it is common in elderly patients, associated with narcotic use and hypoxia. Proper medical optimization and perioperative care are crucial for risk mitigation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old patient presents with increasing pain in the shoulder 3 weeks after a total shoulder arthroplasty. Aspiration yields purulent fluid. What is the most likely responsible organism and the standard surgical management?', '["Staphylococcus aureus; Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR)", "Streptococcus pyogenes; Suppressive intravenous antibiotics only", "Cutibacterium acnes; One-stage exchange arthroplasty", "Staphylococcus epidermidis; Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR)", "Staphylococcus aureus; Two-stage exchange arthroplasty"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Periprosthetic joint infections present differently based on timing. Acute infections (typically < 3-4 weeks) are often caused by highly virulent organisms like Staphylococcus aureus and can sometimes be managed with Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR). Chronic infections are usually due to less virulent organisms like Staph epidermidis and require a two-stage exchange.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old patient presents with severe shoulder pain and limited range of motion. Imaging and clinical evaluation confirm osteoarthritis with an intact rotator cuff. Which of the following is the most appropriate surgical intervention?', '["Arthroscopic debridement and subacromial decompression", "Hemiarthroplasty", "Reverse Total Shoulder Arthroplasty (rTSA)", "Anatomic Total Shoulder Arthroplasty (TSA)", "Arthrodesis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The primary indication for Anatomic Total Shoulder Arthroplasty (TSA) is osteoarthritis with an intact rotator cuff. Anatomic TSA relies on an intact rotator cuff to maintain the humeral head centered on the glenoid. In contrast, rTSA is designed for cuffs that are severely deficient, utilizing the deltoid muscle to substitute for the rotator cuff.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old patient presents with a painful right hip and a palpable soft-tissue mass. They underwent a metal-on-metal total hip arthroplasty 10 years ago. Serum cobalt and chromium levels are elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Iliopsoas bursitis", "Heterotopic ossification", "Aseptic loosening due to polyethylene wear", "Periprosthetic joint infection", "Adverse local tissue reaction (ALTR) / Pseudotumor"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Metal-on-metal hip implants can produce metal ion wear debris, which can trigger an adverse local tissue reaction (ALTR), often manifesting as a solid or cystic pseudotumor, pain, and elevated serum metal ions.');
